<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera was a Colombian general who served four times as president. After leading a liberal rebellion in the civil war of 1860–1862, he oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rionegro">Rionegro</a> constitution of 1863, which created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> as a loose federation. He also confiscated church property and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mosquera was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a> on 26 September 1798 into a wealthy landowning family; one brother, Joaquín, became President of Gran Colombia and another, Manuel José, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>. He was a slaveholder and a self-taught mathematician, historian and writer. By 1814 he served in the independence movement under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>. In 1824, fighting the royalist Agustín Agualongo at Barbacoas, he was shot through the lower jaw and needed a metal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prosthesis">prosthesis</a> that left his speech marked by whistling sounds, earning him the mocking nickname Mascachochas. He became a general in 1829.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After diplomatic posts and service as Secretary of War, in which he won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-supremes">War of the Supremes</a> in 1840, he was elected president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-new-granada">Republic of New Granada</a> in 1845 with the support of the faction that later formed the Conservative Party. His government signed the Mallarino-Bidlack Treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1846, abolished remnant colonial taxes, revived the tobacco industry, held the country's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and began separating Church and State. Drawn ever closer to the liberals, he helped defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of José María Melo in 1854.</p><p>As president of the Cauca State he opposed President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-ospina-rodriguez">Mariano Ospina Rodríguez</a>, and in 1860 declared Cauca's secession and made war on the Granadine Confederation, taking power in 1861. In 1863 he personally led the Colombian army to victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. Elected again in 1866 against the opposition of radical liberals, he clashed with the Church, which brought a strong intervention by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a>, and he closed the ordinary sessions of Congress in 1867. A coup removed him on 23 May 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mosquera spent three years in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, where he wrote his Cosmogonía, a study of theories of the creation of the universe. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> by 1871, he failed to win the presidency but led the Cauca State until 1873 and was a senator again in 1876. He died on 7 October 1878 at his farm of Coconuco, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a>, and was buried in the Panteón de los Próceres there. For the liberal reforms carried out under him he is counted among the most important figures of 19th-century Colombia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1860 <b>Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera</b>, governor of the State of Cauca, rebelled and declared Cauca separate from the confederation, starting a civil war.</blockquote><a href="/state/granadine-confederation">Granadine Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>After winning the civil war of 1860 to 1862 against the Granadine Confederation, <b>Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera</b> and the Liberals met at Rionegro in Antioquia and adopted a new constitution in 1863, creating the United States of Colombia, made up of nine sovereign states including Panama.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-of-colombia">United States of Colombia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. Died in Puracé, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=2.45,-76.61&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Popayán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 4790h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 4790h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=2.45,-76.61&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
